T = []
def text(title, topic, body, glossary, qs): T.append(dict(title=title, topic=topic, level="C1", body=body.strip(), glossary=[dict(term=a, pl=b) for a, b in glossary], questions=qs))
def mc(q, opts, a, expl, notes): return dict(task_type="mc", instruction="Wybierz odpowiedź zgodną z tekstem.", prompt=dict(question=q, options=opts), answers=[a], explanation=expl, distractor_notes=notes)
def tf(q, a, expl): return dict(task_type="tfng", instruction="Czy zdanie jest zgodne z tekstem?", prompt=dict(question=q, options=["True", "False", "Not Given"]), answers=[a], explanation=expl)

text("The Quiet Rise of the Four-Day Week", "work", """
When a mid-sized software firm in Leeds announced that its staff would work four days a week for the same salary, many commentators predicted chaos. Clients, they argued, would feel abandoned on Fridays, deadlines would slip, and productivity would inevitably suffer. Two years on, the company reports that none of these fears have materialised. Revenue has grown, staff turnover has halved, and the number of sick days has fallen to its lowest level since records began.

The managing director, Helen Okafor, is careful not to present the scheme as a miracle cure. "We didn't simply remove a day and hope for the best," she explains. "We spent six months examining how people actually used their time. We found that a remarkable proportion of the week was swallowed up by meetings that had no clear purpose." Meetings were capped at thirty minutes, internal email was restricted to certain hours, and teams were encouraged to agree on periods of uninterrupted work.

Critics point out that such arrangements are far easier to introduce in knowledge-based industries than in sectors such as healthcare or retail, where a physical presence is required and staff cannot simply compress their work into fewer hours. Some economists also warn that early results may be distorted by what is known as the novelty effect: employees who feel grateful for a perk may work harder at first, but this enthusiasm could fade once the arrangement becomes routine.

Nevertheless, the idea is gaining ground. Several national trials have reported broadly positive outcomes, and a growing number of job advertisements now mention a shorter working week as a benefit. For younger workers in particular, flexibility appears to matter as much as pay. Whether the four-day week will become the norm remains to be seen, but it has already forced many employers to ask an uncomfortable question: how much of the traditional working week is genuinely productive, and how much is simply habit?
""", [("materialise", "urzeczywistnić się"), ("staff turnover", "rotacja pracowników"), ("swallowed up", "pochłonięty"), ("cap", "ograniczyć (górnym limitem)"), ("compress", "skompresować, ścisnąć"), ("novelty effect", "efekt nowości"), ("perk", "dodatkowy benefit"), ("gain ground", "zyskiwać popularność")],
[mc("What does the writer say about the predictions made by commentators?", ["They proved to be unfounded.", "They were partly accurate.", "They were ignored by the company.", "They led to changes in the scheme."], "They proved to be unfounded.", "Akapit 1: 'none of these fears have materialised'.", {"They were partly accurate.": "Tekst mówi, że żadna z obaw się nie spełniła.", "They were ignored by the company.": "Nic o ignorowaniu – firma analizowała czas pracy.", "They led to changes in the scheme.": "Brak informacji o zmianach pod wpływem komentarzy."}),
 mc("According to Helen Okafor, the success of the scheme depended mainly on", ["careful analysis of how time was spent.", "employees agreeing to longer days.", "hiring additional staff.", "reducing the number of clients."], "careful analysis of how time was spent.", "'We spent six months examining how people actually used their time.'", {"employees agreeing to longer days.": "Nie ma mowy o dłuższych dniach pracy.", "hiring additional staff.": "Brak wzmianki o zatrudnianiu.", "reducing the number of clients.": "Tekst o tym nie wspomina."}),
 mc("The 'novelty effect' refers to", ["a temporary increase in effort.", "the difficulty of trying new methods.", "resistance to change among managers.", "the popularity of new technology."], "a temporary increase in effort.", "Pracownicy mogą pracować ciężej na początku, ale entuzjazm może osłabnąć.", {"the difficulty of trying new methods.": "Chodzi o entuzjazm, nie trudność.", "resistance to change among managers.": "Nie dotyczy menedżerów.", "the popularity of new technology.": "Nie dotyczy technologii."}),
 tf("The four-day week can be introduced equally easily in all industries.", "False", "Krytycy wskazują, że w opiece zdrowotnej i handlu jest to znacznie trudniejsze."),
 tf("The company in Leeds has opened a second office since introducing the scheme.", "Not Given", "Tekst nie wspomina o nowym biurze."),
 mc("What is the writer's main purpose in the final paragraph?", ["To suggest the idea challenges assumptions about work.", "To argue that the four-day week should be compulsory.", "To show that young workers care only about pay.", "To predict that the trend will soon end."], "To suggest the idea challenges assumptions about work.", "Końcowe pytanie o to, ile tygodnia pracy jest naprawdę produktywne.", {"To argue that the four-day week should be compulsory.": "Autor nie postuluje obowiązku.", "To show that young workers care only about pay.": "Wręcz przeciwnie – elastyczność jest równie ważna jak płaca.", "To predict that the trend will soon end.": "Autor mówi 'remains to be seen' – brak prognozy końca."})])

text("Why We Trust Strangers Online", "media", """
Every day, millions of people hand over money to someone they have never met, on the strength of a few stars and a handful of comments written by other strangers. A generation ago, this behaviour would have struck most people as reckless. Today it is so routine that we barely notice it. Online reviews have become one of the most powerful forces in modern commerce, shaping where we eat, which hotels we book and even which doctors we visit.

Psychologists explain our reliance on reviews through the concept of social proof: when we are uncertain, we look to the behaviour of others to decide what is correct. In an unfamiliar city, a crowded restaurant seems more appealing than an empty one. Online ratings simply extend this instinct to a global scale. The difficulty is that the crowd we are following is largely invisible. We cannot see whether the reviewers resemble us, whether they were paid, or whether they exist at all.

The problem of fake reviews is well documented. Some businesses pay agencies to post glowing comments about themselves or damaging ones about competitors. Platforms insist that they remove millions of suspicious reviews every year, yet researchers who study the issue believe that a significant proportion still slips through. More subtle distortions also exist. People who have had an extreme experience, whether wonderful or disastrous, are far more likely to write a review than those whose experience was merely adequate. As a result, ratings tend to cluster at the top and bottom of the scale, painting a picture that is more dramatic than reality.

None of this means that reviews are worthless. Read carefully, they can reveal patterns that no advertisement would admit to: a hotel with thin walls, a shop that is slow to give refunds. The key, experts suggest, is to treat ratings as one source of evidence among many, to look for detailed accounts rather than vague praise, and to be wary of any product that appears to have no critics at all.
""", [("on the strength of", "na podstawie"), ("reckless", "lekkomyślny"), ("social proof", "społeczny dowód słuszności"), ("glowing", "entuzjastyczny (o opinii)"), ("slip through", "prześlizgnąć się"), ("adequate", "wystarczający, przeciętny"), ("cluster", "skupiać się"), ("be wary of", "uważać na")],
[mc("In the first paragraph, the writer suggests that trusting online reviews", ["has become normal very quickly.", "was common a generation ago.", "is limited to restaurants.", "is a sign of reckless behaviour today."], "has become normal very quickly.", "Kiedyś uznano by to za lekkomyślne, dziś to rutyna.", {"was common a generation ago.": "Przeciwnie – wtedy wydawałoby się lekkomyślne.", "is limited to restaurants.": "Wymieniono hotele i lekarzy.", "is a sign of reckless behaviour today.": "Autor mówi, że dziś to rutyna."}),
 mc("What problem with 'social proof' online does the writer identify?", ["We know little about the people we are following.", "Crowded places are often of low quality.", "People prefer empty restaurants.", "It works only in unfamiliar cities."], "We know little about the people we are following.", "'The crowd we are following is largely invisible.'", {"Crowded places are often of low quality.": "Tego nie twierdzi.", "People prefer empty restaurants.": "Przeciwnie.", "It works only in unfamiliar cities.": "Przykład z miastem jest ilustracją, nie ograniczeniem."}),
 tf("Platforms claim to delete large numbers of suspicious reviews.", "True", "'Platforms insist that they remove millions of suspicious reviews.'"),
 tf("Most reviews are written by people whose experience was average.", "False", "Częściej piszą osoby z ekstremalnymi doświadczeniami."),
 mc("Why do ratings 'cluster at the top and bottom of the scale'?", ["People with strong experiences are more likely to write.", "Platforms delete average reviews.", "Businesses pay for extreme ratings only.", "Most products are either excellent or terrible."], "People with strong experiences are more likely to write.", "Bezpośrednio z akapitu 3.", {"Platforms delete average reviews.": "Brak takiej informacji.", "Businesses pay for extreme ratings only.": "Płatne opinie to osobny problem.", "Most products are either excellent or terrible.": "Autor mówi, że obraz jest bardziej dramatyczny niż rzeczywistość."}),
 tf("Experts recommend avoiding online reviews altogether.", "False", "Zalecają traktowanie ich jako jednego z wielu źródeł.")])

text("The Forgotten Art of Getting Lost", "travel", """
For most of human history, finding your way through an unfamiliar place required attention, memory and a degree of courage. Travellers studied maps, asked strangers for directions and noticed landmarks: a church spire, a bend in the river, a distinctive shop sign. Today, a small blue dot on a phone screen does all of this for us. We follow it obediently, often without lifting our eyes to look at the streets we are walking through.

Satellite navigation has undoubtedly made travel safer and more efficient. Few people would wish to return to the days of arguing over a folded paper map in a car park. Yet some researchers are beginning to ask what we may have lost in exchange. Studies of the brain suggest that people who navigate using their own sense of direction show greater activity in the hippocampus, a region associated with memory and spatial thinking. When we simply follow instructions, that region appears to be far less engaged.

The concern is not only neurological. Travel writers have long argued that the most memorable moments of a journey are often unplanned: the café discovered after a wrong turn, the conversation with a local who offered directions. By guiding us efficiently from one recommended attraction to the next, navigation apps may be quietly narrowing our experience of new places. We arrive faster, but we may notice less on the way.

Some people have responded by deliberately switching off their phones for part of a trip, or by setting themselves small challenges, such as reaching a destination using only a paper map. Such experiments can be frustrating at first, but many report that they remember these journeys far more vividly. The point is not to reject technology, which would be neither practical nor sensible, but to recognise that efficiency is not the only value worth pursuing when we travel. Occasionally, getting lost may be exactly what we need.
""", [("landmark", "punkt orientacyjny"), ("spire", "iglica"), ("obediently", "posłusznie"), ("hippocampus", "hipokamp"), ("spatial", "przestrzenny"), ("engaged", "zaangażowany, aktywny"), ("narrow", "zawężać"), ("vividly", "żywo, wyraziście")],
[mc("The writer describes the 'small blue dot' in order to show", ["how passive modern navigation has become.", "how inaccurate phone maps are.", "why travellers prefer paper maps.", "how dangerous streets can be."], "how passive modern navigation has become.", "Podążamy za nią posłusznie, nie patrząc na ulice.", {"how inaccurate phone maps are.": "Nie ma mowy o niedokładności.", "why travellers prefer paper maps.": "Tekst mówi raczej odwrotnie.", "how dangerous streets can be.": "Nie dotyczy niebezpieczeństwa."}),
 tf("Using one's own sense of direction is linked to more activity in the hippocampus.", "True", "Tak wynika z badań opisanych w akapicie 2."),
 tf("The writer believes satellite navigation has made travel less safe.", "False", "'has undoubtedly made travel safer'."),
 mc("According to travel writers, memorable moments are often", ["the result of unexpected events.", "found at famous attractions.", "planned in advance.", "shared on social media."], "the result of unexpected events.", "'often unplanned: the café discovered after a wrong turn'.", {"found at famous attractions.": "Aplikacje prowadzą do atrakcji, ale to nie one są najbardziej pamiętne.", "planned in advance.": "Przeciwnie.", "shared on social media.": "Brak wzmianki."}),
 tf("People who use paper maps always arrive at their destination faster.", "Not Given", "Tekst nie porównuje czasu dotarcia przy mapie papierowej."),
 mc("What is the writer's overall position?", ["Technology is useful, but efficiency is not everything.", "Navigation apps should be avoided completely.", "Paper maps are more efficient than phones.", "Getting lost is always a positive experience."], "Technology is useful, but efficiency is not everything.", "'The point is not to reject technology… efficiency is not the only value'.", {"Navigation apps should be avoided completely.": "Autor wyraźnie odrzuca takie podejście.", "Paper maps are more efficient than phones.": "Nie twierdzi tego.", "Getting lost is always a positive experience.": "Mówi 'occasionally'."})])
